# Copy to .env for local development (`cp env.example .env`). .env is
# git-ignored and must never be committed (CLAUDE.md invariant 13). Prod never
# uses env files — OpenBao only.
#
# Named env.example (not .env.example) because the agent harness denies writes
# to any .env* path — the same guard that protects the real .env. `git mv` it
# if the dotted name is preferred.
POSTGRES_USER=intake
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=intake-dev-only
POSTGRES_DB=intake
MINIO_ROOT_USER=intake-dev
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=intake-dev-only
# The OpenBao pointer — the ONLY secret-adjacent values .env may ever hold
# (invariant 13; enforced by tests/security/test_openbao_wiring.py). Real
# secrets live in OpenBao; prod refuses dev-shaped tokens and fails closed.
OPENBAO_ADDR=http://127.0.0.1:8200
OPENBAO_TOKEN=intake-dev-root
#
# services/api dev tier (INT-197). The two dev-bearer tokens are REQUIRED for
# any /v1 request and deliberately have NO default anywhere — the API answers
# 503 until both are set. Export them in your shell; never write values into
# a committed file (the allow-list test above pins that):
#   INTAKE_DEV_TOKEN_PATIENT=<choose-a-value>
#   INTAKE_DEV_TOKEN_ADMIN=<choose-a-value>
# Optional knobs (compose-role defaults apply when unset):
#   INTAKE_API_DB_URL / INTAKE_API_AUDIT_DB_URL / INTAKE_API_BOOTSTRAP_DB_URL
#   INTAKE_API_KEK_MODE=transit|file-dev   (file-dev = process-local KEKs,
#     notes do not survive a restart — throwaway runs only)
#   MINIO_ENDPOINT / INTAKE_DOCS_BUCKET / INTAKE_AUDIT_BUCKET
#
# Document ingest needs an AV verdict or it fails closed (503), so adding a
# record is impossible until one of the knobs below is exported. Prefer the
# first: INT-120's ClamAV client is wired whenever a clamd endpoint is
# configured, and `make dev` starts clamd on 127.0.0.1:3310. There is
# deliberately NO default endpoint — a guessed scanner is a scan that did not
# happen — so this must be set explicitly, in your shell:
#   INTAKE_CLAMD_HOST=127.0.0.1              (REQUIRED for document upload)
#   INTAKE_CLAMD_PORT=3310                   (default; clamd's IANA port)
#   INTAKE_CLAMD_UNIX_SOCKET=/var/run/clamav/clamd.sock  (instead of the host)
#   INTAKE_CLAMD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=<seconds>
#   INTAKE_DEV_ALLOW_UNSCANNED=explicit-yes  (INT-034 gate: the "mark
#     everything clean" dev stub, reachable ONLY when no clamd is configured
#     — a real scanner always wins over it)
#
# INT-202 backups (infra/docker-compose.backup.yml, profile `backup`). Names
# only — the five credentials come from OpenBao and never from an env file,
# and the allow-list test above permits no new key here:
#   PGBACKREST_REPO1_CIPHER_PASS       repository encryption passphrase
#   PGBACKREST_REPO1_S3_KEY            repository object-store access key
#   PGBACKREST_REPO1_S3_KEY_SECRET     repository object-store secret
#   INTAKE_DR_S3_ENDPOINT              the second location; NO default exists
#   INTAKE_DR_S3_ACCESS_KEY / INTAKE_DR_S3_SECRET_KEY
# Two knobs with no value anyone has supplied yet:
#   PGBACKREST_REPO1_RETENTION_FULL    how many full backups to keep. files/10
#     §3 states RPO 15 min and RTO 4 h and states no retention window, so the
#     drill refuses without this (files/HUMAN_BLOCKERS.md HB-I8).
#   INTAKE_CONTAINER_MEMORY_BYTES      what the container runtime may use; the
#     drill's host preflight reads it (HB-I2).
# Read by services/probes and already defaulted there:
#   INTAKE_BACKUP_DIR=.cache/backups   where drill evidence lands
#   INTAKE_BACKUP_MAX_AGE_S=900        RPO 15 min, as the freshness bound

# INT-032 openEHR export tier (infra/docker-compose.dev.yml, profile `export`).
# The CDR address is not a secret and has NO default in code — a guessed CDR is
# a clinical record written somewhere nobody looked. The credentials are NOT
# here and never will be: intake_platform.ehrbase reads them from OpenBao
# `secret/ehrbase`, which scripts/security/openbao_dev_setup.sh seeds with the
# same dev pair the compose service is configured with. Export in your shell:
#   INTAKE_EHRBASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8092/ehrbase
#   INTAKE_EHRBASE_HEALTH_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8092/ehrbase/management/health
#     (wires the `ehrbase` synthetic probe; unset, that registry row reports
#     SKIPPED, which is the honest state while no deployment runs the tier)
# Overriding the dev pair means overriding it in BOTH places, because compose
# and the vault have to agree:
#   EHRBASE_USER / EHRBASE_PASSWORD                the CDR's BASIC auth pair
#   EHRBASE_ADMIN_USER / EHRBASE_ADMIN_PASSWORD
#   EHRBASE_DB_USER / EHRBASE_DB_PASSWORD / EHRBASE_DB_ADMIN_USER /
#   EHRBASE_DB_ADMIN_PASSWORD / EHRBASE_DB_ROOT_PASSWORD

# `make clips VOICE=<voice_id>` (INT-060) pre-synthesises the compiled clip
# manifests into the MinIO clip cache. It needs exactly ONE source of speech
# and guesses neither — the clamd rule again, for the same reason: a guessed
# TTS is a clip nobody synthesised. Set one of these, in your shell:
#   INTAKE_TTS_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8803   (a self-hosted TTS speaking
#     POST /synthesize -> 16-bit PCM RIFF/WAVE; the team GPU lanes are reached
#     over the SSH forward, docs/runbooks/team-gpu-server.md. Neither lane is
#     a dependency: absent or dead, the build stops with a typed error)
#   INTAKE_TTS_RECORDED_DIR=<dir>               (recorded responses, one JSON
#     per scripted string named by the compiler's text_hash — the CI path, and
#     the dev path on a machine with no GPU)
# Optional:
#   INTAKE_CLIPS_BUCKET=intake-clips            (default; a separate bucket
#     from the documents one, holding agent speech and no patient data)
